1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,039
Suppose a teammate says, "This class only
has a byte field, so each instance is
basically one byte." Then they multiply by
ten million instances, glance at the heap,
and wonder why reality refused the
arithmetic.

2
00:00:13,159 --> 00:00:22,519
Episode Sixty-Two taught us to measure.
Object layout is one reason measurements
surprise people who estimate memory by
field sizes alone. Every object pays for a
header.

3
00:00:22,639 --> 00:00:34,439
On HotSpot, that header carries identity
and metadata the runtime needs — mark-
related information, class pointer
information, and related machinery that
varies with JVM version and configuration.

4
00:00:34,559 --> 00:00:45,199
You do not need every bit field to learn
the lesson: a "tiny" object is never only
its fields. Look at this java for a
moment. Tiny looks like one byte of
payload.

5
00:00:45,319 --> 00:00:56,999
Alignment and padding round the instance
size up to the platform's object alignment
— commonly eight bytes on many
configurations. One byte of business data
can sit inside a much larger footprint.

6
00:00:57,119 --> 00:01:09,999
Create millions of Tiny instances and you
have paid for millions of headers and
padding gaps. Arrays add their own story:
array headers include length, then
elements, still subject to alignment.

7
00:01:10,119 --> 00:01:20,799
A byte[] is not simply "length bytes" in
retained-size accounting. Empty arrays are
not free either — thousands of empty
collections "just in case" pay header
taxes repeatedly.

8
00:01:20,919 --> 00:01:29,679
Sometimes a shared empty singleton or null
meaning empty is denser — with care for
mutability. References complicate the
arithmetic further.

9
00:01:29,799 --> 00:01:41,799
A field of type String is not "the
string's characters inside your object."
It is a reference — a pointer-sized slot,
often compressed — plus whatever the
String itself costs elsewhere.

10
00:01:41,919 --> 00:01:56,239
Ignoring reference costs is how people
underestimate graphs of wrappers, tree
nodes, and DTOs. Compressed oops use
narrower references on heaps in a certain
size range, trading a little decode work
for denser memory.

11
00:01:56,359 --> 00:02:08,039
On many heaps, references cost less than a
full 64-bit pointer — which is also why
whiteboard "pointer is eight bytes"
estimates can be wrong either way. When
you need precision, measure.

12
00:02:08,159 --> 00:02:19,919
Field order can matter for packing. The
JVM lays out fields with alignment rules;
reordering source fields can change
padding gaps. This is real — and a footgun
for premature obsession.

13
00:02:20,039 --> 00:02:25,919
Algorithms, data-structure choice, and
retention policy usually dominate before
field packing saves you.

14
00:02:26,039 --> 00:02:35,120
Use layout knowledge to interpret heap
numbers and to decide when many tiny
objects should become denser
representations — arrays of primitives,
fewer wrappers,

15
00:02:35,239 --> 00:02:41,799
flyweight-style sharing — not as an excuse
to rearrange fields while an unbounded
cache still grows.

16
00:02:41,919 --> 00:02:53,879
Bring Tiny into a product decision. A
telemetry pipeline boxes every metric
sample into a small object with a few
fields and a reference to a name string.
Ten million samples later, headers and
references dominate.

17
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:07,479
The fix might be a columnar structure —
parallel arrays of primitives and a
dictionary for names — not a field reorder
on the small class. Layout knowledge
showed why the heap exploded; data
representation fixed it.

18
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:14,840
So why is a tiny object bigger than
expected? Headers, alignment, and
references add overhead. Say that in an
interview.

19
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:25,800
Then say what you would do next: measure
retained sizes with a heap dump or
instrumentation, ask whether the design
needs millions of tiny objects at all, and
only then worry about packing.

20
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:36,320
Estimating by field sizes only, ignoring
reference costs, and layout-obsessing
before algorithms are the three mistakes
this episode exists to prevent. Object
layout makes the heap's accounting honest.

21
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:46,000
But pauses are not only about how big
objects are. Sometimes latency appears
when the JVM needs all threads to reach a
safe state before a VM operation.

22
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:54,320
Those rendezvous points are safepoints —
and time-to-safepoint can show up as
mysterious latency even when "GC was slow"
is not the whole story.
